@* What the microphone is hearing, right now. Somebody who cannot see this cannot tell a silent room from a dead
   microphone, and they will keep talking into either one.

   The level itself is a CSS custom property published by whichever recorder is running — mic.js on a spoken turn,
   live-audio.js during a fight — so the bar is painted by the browser rather than pushed a frame at a time. The
   eight bars behind it are the same thing with the frequencies kept apart: a level says how loud, a shape says it
   is a voice and not the fan.

   T110: when the bar crosses into the danger zone, sparks are thrown from the lamp's corner. The level itself
   never leaves the browser — a tiny observer in <c>js/level-clip.js</c> watches <c>--po-mic-level</c> and asks
   <c>PoParticles.burst</c> to fire on the rising edge, then again whenever the level has stayed above the
   threshold past a short cooldown. The bar paint path is unchanged. *@
<div class="meter @(Listening ? "meter--on" : null)" role="status" aria-label="@Label">
    <span class="lamp" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="caption po-eyebrow">@Caption</span>
    <div class="track" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="bands">
            @for (var band = 0; band < Bands; band++)
            {
                <span class="band"></span>
            }
        </div>
        <div class="fill"></div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>How many bars the spectrum draws. The recorders publish exactly this many custom properties.</summary>
    private const int Bands = 8;

    [Parameter] public bool Listening { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the meter is for, in words — it is also what a screen reader is told.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Caption { get; set; } = "Listening";

    private string Label => Listening ? $"{Caption}. The microphone is open." : "The microphone is off";
}
